<%# The one bar every screen wears. DESIGN.md has called `.masthead` "one bar for
    every screen" since the design system was written; until story 0011 the code
    was six copies of it that happened to agree, and the brand string was typed
    out in about fifteen places. Renaming the product is what presented the bill.

    Three questions, top to bottom: what this is (the brand), where you are (the
    label), where else you can go (the compass). One fact rides on the right.

    Two variances this partial carries rather than flattens:

      brand   ├── <a href=root_path>  every screen a reader can leave
              └── <span>              the empty state — there is no archive to
                                      go back to yet, so the brand is not a door

      compass ├── rendered            every screen but one
              └── suppressed          /feed, which carries its own sticky
                                      `.rail` instead. Two `<nav>`s labelled
                                      "Sections" on one page is a defect, not a
                                      redundancy — see paintings/index.

    Locals:
      label:    required. What this screen is.
      here:     which of the four surfaces this screen is, or nil when it is
                none of them (`/days/:date`, the 404). ApplicationHelper#
                compass_destinations owns the contract and raises on anything
                that is neither nil nor one of its keys.
      compass:  default true. False renders no compass at all — /feed only.
      linked:   default true. False renders the brand as a span.
      aside:    optional string, wrapped in `.masthead__aside` here.
      aside_datetime: optional ISO 8601 date. Present, the aside is a <time>
                with that `datetime`; absent, a <span>. Only the daily page
                passes it — "13 August 2026" is a date and should say so in
                markup, "110 works · Mia" is not.

    There was an `aside_html:` local until story 0012. Only the daily page used
    it, to `capture` a <time> whose date was sometimes a link into the archive.
    The compass now carries that door with the word `Days` on it, unconditionally,
    so what is left is a date with no link in it — one more local, and no
    pre-built markup crossing a partial boundary.

    Deliberately NOT a `render layout:` block. `yield` inside a partial is
    `_layout_for`, which silently returns the surrounding buffer when the
    partial was rendered without `layout:` — so a plain `render "shared/masthead"`
    produced no aside and raised nothing. Explicit locals beat one clever one
    that fails quietly.
%>
<header class="masthead">
  <% if local_assigns.fetch(:linked, true) %>
    <a class="masthead__brand" href="<%= root_path %>">Tondo</a>
  <% else %>
    <span class="masthead__brand">Tondo</span>
  <% end %>

  <p class="masthead__label"><%= label %></p>

  <%# The corner (story 0017). The reader's own room, on every screen that wears
      this bar — eight of them, seven with a compass.

      NOT ON THE COMPASS ROW, and the reason is arithmetic rather than taste.
      At 375px with Dynamic Type at its cap the four labels measure 270.23px and
      the gap clamp floors to its 0.7rem minimum of 14px, so the row is 312.23
      of the 329.0px the page leaves. A fifth item needs 44 + 14 = 58px against
      16.77px of spare and wraps, and a wrapped row costs 44px straight out of a
      fold budget the front door clears by 9px. `test/system/favorites_test.rb`
      asserts four caps-links, one row, and a 10px adjacency floor — three ways
      to fail. Here it shares the vertical band the brand and label rows already
      occupy and adds no height at the cap.

      THE MARK IS THE OCULUS — the centre of the rose window the App Store icon
      draws. `DESIGN.md` says the mark appears "nowhere inside the app", and the
      amendment there records why this does not trip it: the objection is the
      gilt FIELD, a filled gold square on the calmest surface in the product.
      This is its inverse — a linen field with a gold line, the same ink weight
      as the keep bookmark that has been inside the app since story 0006.

      PURE VIEW LOGIC ONLY. `pages/privacy` and `pages/support` render this bar,
      and `PagesController` inherits `ActionController::Base` rather than
      `ApplicationController` (deliberately — see that file). `current_user`,
      `current_device` and `native_shell?` are all UNDEFINED there, so a helper
      call here is a 500 on the two pages App Review fetches anonymously.
      `current_page?` is ActionView and is safe.

      It also has to stay identical for every reader: this bar renders inside
      `/`, which is `Cache-Control: public` behind Thruster. A corner that
      showed a signed-in state would put per-visitor markup into a shared cache.
      There is no state here and there must never be. %>
  <% if current_page?(corner_path) %>
    <span class="masthead__you masthead__you--here" aria-current="page">
      <%= render "shared/oculus" %>
      <span class="visually-hidden">Your corner</span>
    </span>
  <% else %>
    <a class="masthead__you" href="<%= corner_path %>" aria-label="Your corner">
      <%= render "shared/oculus" %>
    </a>
  <% end %>

  <% if local_assigns[:aside].present? %>
    <% if local_assigns[:aside_datetime].present? %>
      <time class="masthead__aside" datetime="<%= aside_datetime %>"><%= aside %></time>
    <% else %>
      <span class="masthead__aside"><%= aside %></span>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# `fetch` with no default: a screen that renders a compass has to say where
      it is, and `here: nil` has to be typed out. Reading it as `local_assigns[:here]`
      would turn a forgotten local into a silent "nothing is marked", which is
      the same defect the helper raises about, one level up. %>
  <% if local_assigns.fetch(:compass, true) %>
    <%# `locked:` is `/you`'s alone — see `compass_destinations`. It must never
        reach a publicly cached page.

        `Array()` rather than `fetch(:locked, [])`: `fetch`'s default only fires
        when the key is ABSENT, and `/you` always passes the key. A nil
        `@locked` would reach the helper and raise on `nil - COMPASS_KEYS`, a
        500 on the one page a bounced visitor lands on. %>
    <%= render "shared/compass", here: local_assigns.fetch(:here),
          locked: Array(local_assigns[:locked]) %>
  <% end %>
</header>
